From 9320f76923bafc3fe3a532262acb49ed6f238738 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 18 Jun 2026 14:40:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 02600/11362] [outer-loop] config paradigm: generic target
 induction MEASURED to fail (3 approaches) -- it is the research frontier

Attempted the config/toggle target-pattern induction paradigm (the #1 backlog lever, 9 games). Measured
THREE generic target-induction approaches, ALL fail on the config games:
  1. stumble (click-config BFS): intractable -- 40-210-cell config space, cannot stumble a win.
  2. shape-matched static target: fails 4/5 -- no static region matches the editable region's shape
     (lf52/s5i5/sc25/g50t; only dc22, likely spurious). Config games have no generic edit-to-match-
     this-reference layout.
  3. systematic editable-cell sweep: fails all 5 -- 200-964 clicks cycling editable colors never wins.

CONCLUSION: config target-pattern induction has no simple generic solution. The win needs to KNOW the
specific target (a pattern, a rule like tr87's glyph-rewrite, a multi-path condition like sc25), which
cannot be induced from generic heuristics. This is the genuine research frontier -- scene-understanding
(read the target/rule from the layout) or learned/per-game reasoning, the same hard-tail conclusion as
the combinatorial class. The click tier worked because stumbling sufficed; config does NOT yield to
stumbling and has NO generic target signal. Did not ship a generic solver (would help ~0 games).
results/arc3_config_paradigm_probe.json. Adversarial-verify: clean.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
